{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVkCT517b9Wg5dsXu83h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user is asking about what happens if they shut down their laptop while a `p9` wait operation (from the P9 skill) is running in the background. They're concerned about the wait job dying silently and not resuming, which has happened to them before with other background jobs.\n\nThis is a good and practical question about the reliability and durability of the P9 skill. Let me think about what the P9 skill documentation says about this:\n\nFrom the system reminder about the P9 skill:\n\"P9 — Broomva productive-wait primitive (the wait optimizer). Convert any blocking external operation — PR CI checks, push-triggered deploys, builds, long-running index ops — into work on the next priority. The reference implementation is a PR CI watcher: drains a context-scoped deferred-work queue while `gh pr checks --watch` runs in the background, classifies failures, and self-heals known categories. Non-PR waits get the same lifecycle via `p9 wait-for` (deploy readiness, long extractions — poll a predicate command with heartbeats and re-arm). Every watcher and wait upholds the termination invariant (BRO-1701): on success, failure, OR kill it reports state + next action and pushes through the configured notify channels — killed watchers never die silently; `p9 stuck-scan` catches live-but-wedged ones.\"\n\nThe key line here is: \"killed watchers never die silently; `p9 stuck-scan` catches live-but-wedged ones.\"\n\nThis suggests that P9 has mechanisms to:\n1. Report state and next action when a wait terminates (including on kill)\n2. Has a stuck-scan feature to catch wedged processes\n\nHowever, the user is asking a very practical question about laptop shutdown - what happens if they literally shut down their computer? Does the wait survive? Does it resume?\n\nI should be honest here: I don't have complete internal knowledge of how P9 works under the hood. \n\nThe documentation provides high-level guarantees about the termination invariant and stuck-scan capabilities, but doesn't explicitly address what happens during a hard shutdown scenario. A laptop shutdown is fundamentally different from a normal process termination—it's an abrupt halt rather than a graceful exit. I should acknowledge this distinction and recommend they check the actual P9 implementation details, reach out to the P9 team directly, or use `p9 stuck-scan` when they boot back up to detect any processes that may have been interrupted.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6935,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6935},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"b0323bae-99a1-4439-9acb-c18473f8e336","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:49:47.309Z","request_id":"req_011CdVkCSE9ZnafhDfSoJASS"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_011CdVkCT517b9Wg5dsXu83h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good paranoia. Honest answer: I don't have the full internal details on what happens to a `p9 wait-for` process during a laptop shutdown specifically.\n\nThe skill doc promises one thing clearly — the \"termination invariant (BRO-1701)\": *on success, failure, **or kill**, it reports state + next action and pushes through notify channels.* It also mentions `p9 stuck-scan` to catch live-but-wedged processes. That suggests P9 has fault-tolerance built in, but \"laptop off\" is a harder shutdown than the normal termination cases those promises cover.\n\nWhat I'd do before relying on it for anything mission-critical:\n\n1. **Check the actual P9 docs** — there's probably more detail on persistence/resumption than the skill listing gives\n2. **Test it yourself** — spin up a wait on something non-critical, yank the power (or just `sudo shutdown -h now`), then check what happens when you boot back up. Does the wait resume? Does `p9 stuck-scan` find it? What does the notification say?\n3. **Ask the P9 team directly** if the docs aren't clear — they'll know exactly what survives a shutdown and what doesn't\n\nThe fact that it explicitly mentions \"never die silently\" and has a stuck-scan feature is a good sign, but \"good sign\" isn't the same as \"I've verified it works across shutdowns.\" For something you'd regret losing (a long CI run you're waiting on), test it first or design around it (maybe don't rely on the wait to auto-resume if you're about to close the laptop).\n\nWhat's the scenario you're thinking about?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6935,"cache_read_input_tokens":17536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6935},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"REDACTED","uuid":"4455d05a-da93-49fd-80cd-667dabe3f099","timestamp":"2026-07-29T04:49:50.727Z","request_id":"req_011CdVkCSE9ZnafhDfSoJASS"}
